<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">K'iche' kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">1225 CE – 1524 CE (approx.) · capital Q'umarkaj</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The most powerful Postclassic Maya kingdom of the Guatemalan highlands, whose lords compiled the Popol Vuh, the great Maya book of creation.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>According to their own chronicles, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiche-people">K'iche'</a> descended from warrior lineages that arrived in the highlands from the east, probably from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-coast-of-mexico">Gulf Coast</a> region, and conquered local peoples in the 13th century. They built fortified capitals and, in the 15th century, founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qumarkaj">Q'umarkaj</a> (Utatlán) on a defensible plateau.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Four great lineages shared power, headed by the Kaweq, whose leaders held the titles Ajpop and Ajpop K'amha. Under K'iq'ab, in the mid-15th century, the kingdom reached its greatest extent, ruling much of western <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-guatemala">Guatemala</a> and the Pacific slope, before revolts, including the secession of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kaqchikel-kingdom">Kaqchikel kingdom</a>, weakened it. Temples, palaces and a ballcourt filled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qumarkaj">Q'umarkaj</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1524 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-de-alvarado">Pedro de Alvarado</a> defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiche-people">K'iche'</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quetzaltenango">Quetzaltenango</a>, where tradition says the hero <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tecun-uman">Tecun Uman</a> fell, then burnt the kings <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxib-keh">Oxib Keh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beleheb-tzi">Beleheb Tz'i</a> and destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qumarkaj">Q'umarkaj</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/popol-vuh">Popol Vuh</a> was written down in the 1550s, and K'iche' remains <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala's</a> most widely spoken Maya language.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kiche-kingdom.jpg" alt="K'iche' kingdom" width="320" height="343"><figcaption>Image: Maunus at English Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:K%27ichekingdom.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1225 CE – 1524 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Q'umarkaj</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>K'iq'ab, Oxib Keh, Beleheb Tz'i</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Maya religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>K'iche'</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/guatemala">Guatemala</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Quiché kingdom, K'iche' Kingdom of Q'umarkaj, Utatlán</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1374&amp;focus=kiche-kingdom" aria-label="K'iche' kingdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2189 1619 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where K'iche' kingdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2509 1875L4910 989M2509 1875L2518 1892M2509 1875L2512 1885M2509 1875L2502 1863"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4910 989h0M2518 1892h0M2512 1885h0M2502 1863h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2509 1875h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2509 1875h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1374&amp;focus=kiche-kingdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
